snifdog -- the SNIF CA Transparency watchdog


WHY IT EXISTS

SNIF's security model makes one load-bearing promise (guarantee #3): the relay
cannot silently impersonate a device, because any certificate mis-issued for a
SNIF hostname appears in public Certificate Transparency (CT) logs, making relay
or CA misbehavior publicly detectable. That promise rests on CT actually being
monitored. snifdog is the monitor -- it turns "publicly detectable" into a
running detector that barks. Without it, guarantee #3 is a claim; with it, it is
an operating control.


WHAT IT DETECTS

A publicly-trusted certificate issued for a SNIF hostname or wildcard whose
private key the legitimate device does not hold -- a rogue/coerced public CA, a
compromised relay standing up its own ACME flow, or any mis-issuance in the SNIF
namespace. It does not try to detect device/key compromise or app-layer attacks.


HOW IT WORKS -- pin the SPKI, not the fingerprint

SNIF binds one key to one name for life: a device never re-keys; a new key gets
a new snif-xxxx name (the cert path reuses the stored CSR on every renewal). So
a name's SubjectPublicKeyInfo (SPKI) is immutable -- across the ~75-day renewal
cycle only the serial and validity window change. snifdog therefore pins the
SHA-256 of the DER SPKI (the RFC 7469 construction):

  - a legitimate renewal (same key, new serial)         -> silent, no alarm
  - a key-substitution impersonation (same name, new key) -> fires immediately

There is no legitimate re-key, so there is no rotation false-positive class.


RUN MODES  (config SNIFDOG_MODE)

  watch      Independent watcher (default). Watches CT for the namespace and
             alerts on any observed SPKI outside the pin set. Needs no access to
             the CA-proxy ledger, so a device owner or an unrelated third party
             can run it -- trusting neither the relay nor the CA-proxy operator.
             This is the only configuration that actually delivers guarantee #3.

  reconcile  Operator enrichment, co-located with the CA proxy. Cross-checks
             every CT observation against the CA-proxy CSR set (csr/ under
             $SNIF_CA_PROXY) to auto-classify the operator's own reissues and
             catch external-CA compromise. (csr/ is the authoritative key set:
             the proxy issues every cert from its csr/<name>.csr and never
             re-keys, so csr/ already holds every legitimate key -- the delivered
             crt//arc/ certs add nothing and are not consulted.) A malicious
             operator can silence their own snifdog, so this complements
             independent watchers.

  both       watch semantics enriched with the CSR-set cross-check.


ANCHORING THE PIN SET

  First-seen anchor (default). On first run snifdog scans the full CT history of
  the namespace and pins one SPKI per name. Since SNIF never re-keys, legitimate
  history holds exactly one SPKI per name; a differing SPKI later -- on the name
  or on an overlapping up-level wildcard -- is a collision and alerts. A
  pre-existing two-SPKI collision in history is reported on the first run.

  Explicit pin (strongest, device side). The device holds its own cert and key,
  so it can anchor from them directly, needing no relay/ledger/CA trust:

      snifdog pin mydevice.snif-1a2b.snif.xyz --from /etc/snif/snif.crt

  CA-proxy CSR ground truth (relay-operator side). Run co-located with the SNIF
  CA proxy (SNIF_CA_PROXY set, its csr/ present), snifdog reads the one CSR the
  proxy keyed for each relay-local name -- csr/<apex>.csr -- as that name's
  authoritative SPKI. Since a SNIF device never re-keys, that CSR holds the only
  key the name may ever present, so the operator anchors every device it serves
  from ground truth it already holds -- no per-device cert, no first-use guess.
  A first-seen cert matching the CSR is recorded as `csr-verified`. Two things
  are *proven* mis-issuances and alert immediately, even on a name never seen
  before: a cert carrying any other key for a relay-local name, and any cert at
  all for a relay-local device zone the proxy holds no CSR for -- issuance always
  loads csr/<zone>.csr first, so a cert for an unprovisioned zone was never
  requested through us. (Infra names listed in SNIFDOG_NAME_ALLOW -- the init
  domain, pool host, base apex -- are not device zones; snifdog ignores them
  entirely and they never reach these checks.) This is name-bound (per-name, not
  an issuer-wide key set) and runs whenever the csr/ store is present, independent
  of SNIFDOG_MODE -- so it is stronger than, and additive to, the reconcile
  CSR-set membership cross-check below.


ON DETECTION

For each new CT cert whose SANs intersect the watched namespace, snifdog
extracts the SANs and SPKI hash and:

  0. For a relay-local name (CSR ground truth available): the cert's key differs
     from that name's CA-proxy CSR, or the proxy holds no CSR for a device zone
     it is authoritative for -> ALERT immediately, a proven mis-issuance, even if
     the name was never seen and never anchored.
  1. SPKI in the pin set for a matching name  -> OK (known-good renewal), silent.
  2. else, SPKI proven legit by the name's CA-proxy CSR, or (reconcile mode)
     present in the CA-proxy CSR set -> auto-learn the pin, low-severity notice.
  3. else -> ALERT: a cert for a SNIF name with an unrecognized key.

An alert emits a structured syslog line (LOG_DAEMON/LOG_CRIT), a non-zero exit
(so the cron wrapper surfaces it), optional webhook POST and e-mail, and an
evidence bundle under evidence/ (cert.der / cert.pem / cert.txt / meta.json) --
proof-grade, because CT is non-repudiable. Duplicate alerts for a bad cert
lingering in the log are suppressed via var/alerted.


CT DATA SOURCE  (config SNIFDOG_SOURCE, pluggable adapters in lib/Snif/CT/)

  certspotter  (default) -- returns cert DER inline and pages via a persistent,
               monotonic after=<id> issuance cursor. Ideal for a watcher: DER in
               one call, no second fetch, resumable. An optional API token
               (SNIFDOG_CERTSPOTTER_TOKEN) raises the free-tier rate limit.

  crtsh        (fallback) -- one whole-history query; the DER for each entry
               needs a second ?d=<id> fetch. No server cursor, so the "cursor"
               is a max-seen id high-water mark and each pass re-fetches + diffs.

The pin DB is a pure function of CT history: given the same logs, a full re-scan
reconstructs the same anchors and collisions. The cursor (var/<source>.cursor)
is only an optimization -- if the source rejects or has pruned it, snifdog
restarts from the beginning and reconverges. (M2 will add direct CT log polling
with STH-signature + Merkle-consistency verification, dropping the aggregator
trust; M3, connector self-watch.)


USAGE

  snifdog pin <name> [--from <cert>|--spki <hex>]   anchor a name's SPKI
  snifdog poll                                       one incremental pass (cron)
  snifdog watch                                      long-running loop
  snifdog list                                       show the pin database


REQUIREMENTS

  Perl core (JSON::PP, Sys::Syslog, File::Temp, File::Path, POSIX, MIME::Base64)
  LWP::UserAgent + LWP::Protocol::https   (HTTPS to the CT source)
  openssl                                 (DER SPKI SHA-256, RFC 7469 pin)


INSTALL / RUN

  snifdog is a standalone sub-package -- it is NOT built or installed by the
  top-level snif `make install` (like ca-proxy, it ships its own configure.ac).
  Build and install it on its own, from this directory:

      cd snifdog && ./configure && make install

  That installs bin/snifdog -> /usr/bin, the snifdog.8 man page, the lib/Snif/CT
  adapters -> /usr/share/snifdog/lib and the systemd units, makes the snifdog
  user if it does not exist, and creates /var/lib/snif/snifdog/{pins,var,evidence}.
  snifdog runs as snifdog and owns its state, which no group can write, so
  that the CA proxy it watches cannot change its pins. On a relay host the
  user is in group snif, the CA proxy's read-only group, for /etc/snif and
  etc/*.domains (ca-proxy/README, "Users and groups"); a standalone watcher
  gets a group of its own. An install over one made before 1.10 gives the
  state to snifdog.

  It configures and runs nothing: no configuration, no cron entry, and no
  unit enabled. The example configuration and cron entry go to
  /usr/share/snifdog/ (snifdog.conf.example, cron.d-snifdog.example).
  Before 1.10 a first install put them in /etc/snif/snifdog.conf and
  /etc/cron.d/snifdog, where the cron entry ran at once, as snif; an
  install leaves them there, so remove the cron entry if you use the units,
  or change its user to snifdog if you keep it. It does not pull in the
  Perl runtime dependencies (see REQUIREMENTS) -- install those from your
  distro, e.g. on Debian/Ubuntu:

      apt install libwww-perl liblwp-protocol-https-perl

  One configuration watches one base domain: one file per base in
  /etc/snif/snifdog.d/, e.g. snif.xyz.conf and vesmail.xyz.conf, from the
  example (SNIFDOG_BASE, SNIFDOG_NAME_ALLOW, sinks). Then run each either as
  a unit, snifdog watch:

      systemctl enable --now snifdog@snif.xyz

  or from cron, snifdog poll every 15 minutes for every configuration,
  by make install-cron, which puts the cron entry in /etc/cron.d/snifdog
  where absent -- not both.
  /etc/snif/snifdog.conf, from before snifdog.d, still works: snifdog.service
  runs it, and the cron entry includes it.

  Each configuration needs its own state directory: the CT cursors and the
  backfill marker under var/ belong to one base's query. A configuration in
  snifdog.d defaults to /var/lib/snif/snifdog/<base>; snifdog.conf keeps
  /var/lib/snif/snifdog. snifdog records the base in var/base and refuses a
  directory another base owns (exit 78). A pass also locks its directory, so
  a cron pass that finds the previous one still running skips quietly.

  To move a one-base install into snifdog.d, move (do not copy) snifdog.conf
  to snifdog.d/<base>.conf and set SNIFDOG_DIR=/var/lib/snif/snifdog in it,
  so it keeps its pins and cursors.


CONTENTS

  bin/snifdog          watcher: pin / poll / watch / list
  snifdog.8            man page
  DESIGN.md            full design note
  etc/snifdog.conf     example configuration (namespace, source, mode, sinks)
  etc/systemd/         snifdog.service, snifdog@.service (watch mode)
  etc/cron.d/          snifdog, example cron entry (poll mode, every configuration)
  test/state.t         state directory, lock and cron loop tests
  lib/Snif/CT/         CT source adapters: CertSpotter.pm, CrtSh.pm
  pins/                one SPKI pin record per watched name/wildcard
  var/                 per-source cursor + alerted cache (idempotent, cron-safe),
                       the owning base (base) and the pass lock (lock)
  evidence/            per-detection evidence bundles
  mkdirs               create the runtime directories

See DESIGN.md for the full design note.
